# RexxHTTP line-ending policy
#
# Repository stores text as LF (portable, canonical). On checkout, all
# RexxHTTP sources are materialised as CRLF, because they are edited on
# Windows (Notepad++) via Dropbox and only travel to the Apache/Ubuntu
# box through a mounted folder.
#
# Why CRLF sources are safe:
#
# - On Unix, RexxHTTP.rex is never exec'd directly by the kernel. Apache
#   execs a small wrapper, which Calls RexxHTTP.rex as a sibling; reached
#   by Call, RexxHTTP.rex's CRLF and its own shebang are inert. ooRexx
#   reads CRLF, LF, or mixed line endings by design.
#
# - On Windows, Apache execs RexxHTTP.rex directly, reading its #!rexx
#   shebang via the default ScriptInterpreterSource. CRLF is native there
#   and causes no trouble. No wrapper is used.
#
# The wrapper is the one file that must be LF (Apache execs it by its
# shebang, and a trailing CR would have the kernel look for "rexx\r"). It
# is NOT stored here: pack.rex generates it fresh, in LF, from a
# ::RESOURCE on every build, and .gitignore keeps it out of the repo so
# no second copy can drift. Being generated and ignored, it never passes
# through these attributes at all.

* text=auto

*.cls text eol=crlf
*.rex text eol=crlf
